prompts.body:
Write a follow-up email to someone who has not replied to my earlier message.

Context of my first message: {{original_context}}
Time since I sent it: {{days_since}} days
Recipient and our relationship: {{relationship}}
What I still need from them: {{need}}
Follow-up number: {{follow_up_number}}
Tone: {{tone}}

Rules:
- Keep it under 80 words.
- Do not apologise for "bumping" or "checking in" and do not guilt-trip.
- Add something new: a useful detail, a deadline that matters, or a simpler version of the ask.
- Make replying effortless, ideally answerable in one line, and offer a polite way to say no or redirect me.
- Match the stated tone.

Output format: two versions, labelled "Version A (light touch)" and "Version B (more direct)", each with a subject line and body. Then one line saying which I should send given the follow-up number.

Help me write a message about a difficult topic.

Situation: {{situation}}
Who I am writing to and my relationship with them: {{recipient}}
What I need them to understand or do: {{outcome}}
Things I must not say or promise: {{boundaries}}
Tone: {{tone}}

Write it so that:
- The main point appears in the first two sentences. No long warm-up.
- It states facts and the impact, not assumptions about their motives.
- It acknowledges how the person may feel in one sincere sentence, with no corporate language.
- It ends with a specific next step and, where useful, an invitation to talk.
- It is no longer than 200 words.

Output format:
1. The email, with subject line.
2. "Riskiest phrase": the one line the recipient is most likely to react badly to, and a softer alternative.
3. Whether this should be a call or in-person conversation instead, in one sentence.

Turn my rough notes into a blog post.

Topic: {{topic}}
Reader and what they care about: {{audience}}
Goal of the post: {{goal}}
Voice: {{voice}}
Length: about {{word_count}} words
Search phrase to include naturally, if any: {{keyword}}

My notes (messy, in any order):
{{notes}}

Instructions:
- Use only the facts and opinions in my notes. If you need a statistic or example that is not in the notes, leave a bracketed placeholder like [add data: ...] instead of inventing one.
- Start with a first paragraph that states the problem or the surprising point. No "In today's fast-paced world".
- Use short paragraphs, descriptive subheadings and concrete examples. One idea per section.
- End with a takeaway and one clear next step for the reader.
- Avoid filler phrases, rhetorical question piles and forced enthusiasm.

Output format: a title, a one-line meta description under 155 characters, then the post in Markdown. Finish with a list of any placeholders I need to fill and any notes you could not place.

Create a detailed outline for an article.

Topic: {{topic}}
Format: {{format}}
Reader and what they already know: {{audience}}
The point I want to make: {{thesis}}
Target length: {{word_count}} words
What I already have (research, stories, data): {{material}}

Build the outline so that:
- It opens with a hook idea and states the thesis in a single sentence.
- Each section has a heading, a one-line purpose ("this section proves/explains..."), 2-4 bullet points and a suggested word count.
- The sections build logically: each one answers the question the previous one raises.
- It includes the strongest counterargument and where to answer it.
- It flags where I still need evidence, examples or an expert quote, using "NEEDS:" tags.
- It finishes with a conclusion that does more than summarise.

Output format: thesis line, then the outline in numbered sections, then a list of all NEEDS items ordered by priority. Ask me up to 3 questions first only if the thesis is unclear.

Act as a candid, constructive editor. Critique the draft below.

Type of writing: {{doc_type}}
Intended reader: {{audience}}
What the draft needs to achieve: {{goal}}
What I am most worried about: {{worry}}

Draft:
{{draft}}

Assess these in order: (1) whether the main point is clear within the first paragraph, (2) structure and flow, (3) evidence and specificity, (4) clarity and sentence-level problems, (5) tone fit for the reader, (6) whether it achieves the goal.

Rules:
- Be specific. Quote the exact words you are criticising and explain the problem in a sentence.
- Rank issues by impact: "Fix first", "Fix next", "Polish".
- For every issue give a concrete fix, ideally a rewritten line. Do not rewrite the whole draft.
- Name at least two things that work and should be kept.
- Do not flatter. If a major section should be cut, say so.

Output format: a one-paragraph verdict, strengths, the ranked issues, then a score out of 10 for how well it meets the goal and the single change that would raise it most.

Turn the meeting notes below into a recap that attendees and absentees can act on.

Meeting: {{meeting_name}}
Date: {{date}}
Recap goes to: {{recipients}}
Tone: {{tone}}

Notes or transcript:
{{notes}}

Rules:
- Extract decisions, action items, open questions and key discussion points. Do not invent any.
- Each action item needs an owner and a due date. If either is not stated in the notes, write "owner TBC" or "due date TBC" instead of guessing.
- Separate what was decided from what was only discussed.
- Keep the recap under 250 words and skimmable.
- Do not include side conversations or personal comments.

Output format: an email with a subject line, then these sections: "Decisions", "Action items" (a table with Task, Owner, Due), "Open questions", "Next meeting". Omit any section with nothing in it.

Write a message declining the request below.

The request: {{request}}
Who asked and our relationship: {{requester}}
Why I am declining (share only what I want shared): {{reason}}
What I can offer instead, if anything: {{alternative}}
Tone: {{tone}}
Channel: {{channel}}

Rules:
- Say no in the first or second sentence. Never leave the answer vague or open-ended.
- Thank them once, specifically, without grovelling.
- Give a brief reason only if it helps, and never invent a false excuse.
- If I offered an alternative, make it concrete; if not, do not hint at one.
- Do not over-apologise, and do not leave the door open for negotiation unless I said so.
- Keep it under 90 words, formatted for the channel.

Output format: the message, then two shorter and firmer versions of the first sentence in case I want a stronger no.

Write a LinkedIn post from the material below.

What happened or what I learned: {{story}}
The lesson or point: {{takeaway}}
My role and audience: {{audience}}
Voice: {{voice}}
Length: {{length}}

Guidelines:
- The first line must stop the scroll with a specific fact, a number or a tension. It must make sense on its own, with no vague hook like "Here is what I learned".
- Use short lines and white space. Keep each paragraph to 1-3 sentences.
- Use only details I gave. Do not invent numbers, quotes or results.
- Be modest and honest, with no humble-bragging and no motivational clichés.
- Skip hashtags except for at most 3 relevant ones at the end. No emojis unless the voice calls for them.
- End with a question or invitation that people can answer in one line.

Output format: the post, then two alternative first lines.

Write a cover letter for the role below.

Role and company: {{role}}
Job description highlights: {{job_description}}
My relevant experience and results: {{experience}}
Why this company: {{motivation}}
Tone: {{tone}}
Length: about {{word_count}} words

Instructions:
- Open with a specific reason I am a strong match, not "I am writing to apply for".
- Choose the 2-3 requirements in the job description that matter most and prove each with one real example from my experience, with numbers where I gave them.
- Use only my facts. If a requirement is not covered by my experience, do not fake it; briefly acknowledge the gap or leave it out.
- Show real interest in the company using the motivation I gave. No generic praise.
- Avoid clichés ("team player", "passionate", "fast-paced environment") and long self-descriptions.
- Close with a confident, specific next step.

Output format: the letter in plain paragraphs with a greeting and sign-off, followed by a short list of any gaps between the job description and my experience that I should address in the interview.

Draft a reply to the email below.

Their email:
{{incoming_email}}

What I want to say or decide (rough points are fine): {{my_points}}
My relationship to the sender: {{relationship}}
Tone: {{tone}}
Length: {{length}}

Instructions:
- Answer every question and request in their email, in the order they asked. If you notice something they asked that my points do not cover, list it after the reply instead of making up an answer.
- Do not agree to anything, give dates or quote numbers that I did not provide.
- Mirror their level of formality, but follow my chosen tone where it differs.
- Start with the answer, not with thanks or a restatement of their email.
- Close with a clear next step and who owns it.

Output format: a subject line only if it should change, then the reply, then "Unanswered items" listing anything I need to decide.

Write a status update from the raw notes below.

Reader: {{reader}}
Project or team: {{project}}
Reporting period: {{period}}
Format: {{format}}

My raw notes:
{{notes}}

Rules:
- Lead with overall status as one of: On track, At risk, Off track, with a one-line reason.
- Report results and outcomes, not activity. "Shipped X" beats "worked on X".
- Quantify progress where my notes allow. Do not invent numbers or dates.
- Make blockers specific: what is blocked, who can unblock it and by when.
- List next-period priorities in order, three at most.
- Keep it under 180 words and avoid filler like "just wanted to update you".

Output format: use the requested format, with sections Status, Done, Blockers and asks, Next. Omit empty sections.

Write a reply to an unhappy customer.

Customer's message:
{{complaint}}

What actually went wrong (internal facts): {{what_happened}}
What we can offer: {{remedy}}
What we cannot do: {{limits}}
Brand voice: {{voice}}
Channel: {{channel}}

Write the reply so that:
- It opens by acknowledging the specific problem in plain words, with an apology for it. No "we apologise for any inconvenience".
- It gives a short, honest explanation without excuses or blaming a third party.
- It states exactly what we will do, by when, and who they can contact.
- It does not promise anything beyond what is listed under "What we can offer".
- It matches the customer's emotional level, calm if they are angry and brisk if they are rushed.
- It is under 150 words.

Output format: the reply, then one line noting the single biggest risk in the wording and a safer alternative.

Write a professional bio for me in three lengths.

Name: {{name}}
Current role: {{role}}
Where it will appear: {{use}}
Background and achievements: {{background}}
What I want to be known for: {{positioning}}
Personal detail to include, if any: {{personal}}
Tone: {{tone}}
Person: {{person}}

Rules:
- Use only the facts I gave. Do not invent employers, awards, degrees or numbers.
- Lead with what matters most to the reader at this use, not with chronological history.
- Replace vague phrases ("passionate", "results-driven", "dynamic") with specific evidence.
- The personal detail should be one light touch at the end, only if it fits the tone.
- Make each version stand on its own rather than just truncating the longer one.

Output format:
- Short: 1 sentence (about 25 words), for a byline or social profile.
- Medium: about 80 words, for a speaker page or website.
- Long: about 170 words, for a full profile or press kit.
